From 99e3f0eb1c30b27ebba3c4c05abf0cbb9f80acc7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Edmond <1571649+edmonddantes@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 10:09:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] Drop completion packets that are not this context's ops

A handle passed to another process stays associated with the port of
the context that used it, so that process's own overlapped I/O on the
handle posts to this port, with an OVERLAPPED that is an address in the
other process. The dequeue, the completion pump and the teardown drain
took every packet's OVERLAPPED for one of their ops and wrote through it.

iocp_packet_op() now decides at all three sites. ior posts its own
packets with key 0, which no other process can. A packet with any other
key must name an op slot in one of the context's chunks with a request in
flight (IOCP_OP_IO or IOCP_OP_IO_CANCEL), and each request takes one
packet (packet_taken, cleared as the request starts), so the pump cannot
stage an op twice. A foreign packet whose OVERLAPPED equals the address
of a slot with a request in flight is still taken for that request, and
the request's own packet then for the slot's next request: nothing in a
packet tells them apart. The key is not compared, because an op on a
duplicate handle takes the association over under its own key, and later
ops on the first handle complete under that key.

A dropped packet is not counted. A dequeue that drops one waits again
for the time left; after 64 drops it returns -EAGAIN, so that a peek
returns even while such packets keep coming. The chunk list is published
with release, since the walk takes no lock while the poller thread may
grow the pool. The README notes that such foreign I/O completes for its
process at once and posts its packet here as well, unless its event
handle has the low-order bit set.

test_iocp_backend: overlapped reads issued outside ior on the ring's
pipe handle stand in for the other process. At each of the three sites
their packets are dropped, the reads complete, and the memory past each
OVERLAPPED stays as it was. One 0 ms peek finds the ring's own write
behind three foreign packets, and a peek gives up after 64 of 70. Ops
taking turns on a pipe handle and its duplicate all complete.
